## Submission

When you've completed your work, you MUST submit your changes as a git patch.
Follow these steps IN ORDER, with SEPARATE commands:

Step 1: Create the patch file
If you created any new source files that are part of the requested feature, first run
`git add -N path/to/new_file1 path/to/new_file2` for those new files.
Then run `git diff -- path/to/file1 path/to/file2 > patch.txt` listing only the source
files you modified or created. The `git add -N` command makes git include new files in
the patch with proper headers.
Do NOT commit your changes.

<IMPORTANT>
The patch must only contain changes to the specific source files you modified to implement the requested feature.
New source files are allowed when they are part of the requested public interface or necessary source implementation.
Do not submit newly created files or changes to any of the following files:

- test and reproduction files
- helper scripts, tests, or tools that you created
- installation, build, packaging, configuration, or setup scripts unless they are directly part of the requested feature (you can assume that the environment is already set up for your client)
- binary or compiled files
</IMPORTANT>

Step 2: Verify your patch
Inspect patch.txt to confirm it only contains your intended changes and headers show `--- a/` and `+++ b/` paths.

Step 3: Submit (EXACT command required)
You MUST use this EXACT command to submit:

```bash
echo COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT && cat patch.txt
```

If the command fails (nonzero exit status), it will not submit.

<CRITICAL>
- Creating/viewing the patch and submitting it MUST be separate commands (not combined with &&).
- If you modify patch.txt after verifying, you SHOULD verify again before submitting.
- You CANNOT continue working (reading, editing, testing) in any way on this task after submitting.
</CRITICAL>
